    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "In terms of your personal outcome or grounds for a challenge, **it is unlikely to be legally actionable or direct evidence of discrimination against you**, primarily because the successful candidate was also a man. \n\nHowever, it does provide some insight into the organization's culture and the hiring dynamics at play. Here is a breakdown of what it may signify and how to interpret the situation:\n\n---\n\n### 1. What the Panel Composition Might Signify\n\n* **Lagging DEI Practices:** Modern public-sector best practices typically mandate or strongly encourage diverse interview panels (across gender, background, and department) to mitigate unconscious bias. An all-male panel for a director-level municipal role suggests the municipality may have outdated HR practices or lacks diversity at the executive level.\n* **Affinity and Subjective Bias:** Homogeneous panels can sometimes be prone to \"in-group\" or affinity bias. While this wouldn’t be gender discrimination between two male candidates, panels without demographic diversity can often lean more heavily on subjective impressions, shared cultural references, or an undefined sense of \"fit\" rather than strictly objective criteria.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why the Other Candidate May Have Been Selected\n\nWhile your technical credentials (Master’s degree, professional license, 12 years of municipal experience) appear stronger on paper, director-level hiring in local government often hinges on factors outside of technical expertise:\n\n* **Political & Public Acumen:** Planning directors spend a significant amount of time dealing with contentious council meetings, upset residents, and media scrutiny. The emphasis on the other candidate's \"extensive experience of public consultation\" suggests the municipality prioritized stakeholder and conflict management over technical planning qualifications.\n* **Internal Ties or Familiarity:** It is common in local government for appointments to favor candidates who already have established rapport with council members, senior administration, or local developers.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Two-Week Delay on Feedback\n\nA two-week wait for written feedback in municipal government is standard rather than a sign of evasion. Written feedback for senior roles often requires review by Human Resources or municipal legal counsel before being released to avoid liability.\n\n---\n\n### Suggested Next Steps\n\n1. **Do not raise the panel's gender composition:** Bringing up the all-male panel will likely put HR on the defensive and close off constructive dialogue, especially since the hire was male.\n2. **Send a polite follow-up:** Reach out to the HR contact or panel chair to reaffirm your interest in constructive feedback:\n   > *\"Thank you again for the opportunity to interview for the Director of Planning position. I am following up on my request for written feedback regarding my interview and application. As I continue to advance my career in municipal planning, any insights into areas where I can strengthen my candidacy would be greatly appreciated.\"*\n3. **Ask targeted questions:** If you secure a call or receive feedback, ask specifically what leadership or community consultation competencies they felt tipped the scale, which will help determine whether your future applications should highlight executive communication over technical planning depth.\n",
